## Wave 10J Lessons (2026-04-24)

### Pattern: Rule authoring without retro-apply is a latent time bomb
Writing a new SOP rule (ECON-DIR1, ECON-UD for all pairs, stationarity CSV) applies it prospectively but leaves all prior pairs non-compliant until an explicit retro-apply task is executed. Every new rule must be paired with a retro-apply task filed against the existing pair inventory — not just a SOP text addition.

### Pattern: Schema evolution requires a portfolio-wide re-validation sweep
When winner_summary.schema.json bumped to v1.1.0, the correct response was to re-run validate_schema.py across ALL committed pairs before any new pair work. Quincy's cloud verify should never be the first signal that legacy pairs are broken.

### Pattern: ECON-CP temporal durability gap
Every pair shipped with a tournament winner that was never checked for temporal robustness. A Sharpe of 1.10 OOS concentrated in one episode (e.g., COVID crash) is not the same as a durable signal. ECON-CP1/CP2 now closes this gap — cross-period durability is mandatory for all future pairs.

## 2026-04-24 — Wave 10J/10K Self-Reflection

### What went well
- SOP craftsmanship under fire: Wave 10I.C found 4 failure classes in Evan's lane; self-diagnosed accurately, fixed all 4, translated each into a binding SOP rule in the same session.
- Debate Round 2 intellectual honesty: conceded ECON-DIR1 producer-voice failure immediately, traced exact lines, proposed consumer-gatekeeper rewrite.
- Systemic team analysis in Round 1: named structural problems (undefined "validation" across 4 SOPs, no schema sweep trigger, no smoke-test coverage map).

### ECON-DIR1 producer voice failure root cause
Authored ECON-DIR1 from the perspective of the active fixer at moment of failure, not from the file-ownership graph. The fix was itself a scope violation (writing to a Dana-owned file), and the rule normalized the violation. Did not stress-test the rule against "who owns this file?" before committing.

### CP1 sub-period Sharpe interpretation
Full OOS CP1 Sharpe (0.02) vs tournament Sharpe (1.11) for indpro_xlp: not a calculation error — a methodological mismatch. CP1-A uses sign(signal)×return; tournament uses rolling threshold. CP1 numbers are directional-durability metrics, not strategy performance replications. Every CP1 handoff must include an explicit methodology_note stating this distinction. Ray's narrative must carry the caveat verbatim.

### Open issues
- interpretation_metadata.json lacks formal JSON schema — vocabulary standard without programmatic enforcement.
- BL-LEGACY-WINNER-SUMMARY-SHAPE: 6 legacy pairs missing 7+ required fields. No wave target.
- CP1-A Sharpe calculation ambiguity: monthly vs daily not prescribed in SOP.
- ECON-SD audit is dead-letter: Quincy's SOP does not name the check.

### Key lessons to carry forward
1. Run validate_schema.py against all sidecars after every schema version bump before any new pair work.
2. Stress-test every new rule against "who owns this file?" before committing.
3. CP1 methodology note is mandatory in every Evan→Vera→Ray handoff.

## 2026-04-24 — Commit without push is not a completed deliverable (META-CPD)

In Wave 10J and 10K, agents (including Evan) committed results locally without pushing to `origin main`. This caused Streamlit Cloud to serve stale data, triggering wasted cloud reboots and QA re-verification cycles that consumed team tokens and delayed closure. The root cause: treating `git commit` as the finish line when the finish line is `git push origin main`.

**How to apply:** After every `git commit`, immediately run `git push origin main` before marking any task or wave complete. A commit that has not been pushed is invisible to Cloud and to other agents. This is now codified as META-CPD in `team-coordination.md` and cross-referenced in Evan's SOP under "Git and Handoff Protocol".

## 2026-06-12 — Negative lead-lag results are publishable; encode fragility where consumers can't drop it

busloans_spy: indicator confirmed as LAGGING (reverse-only Granger). Shipped the honest verdict instead of torturing transforms; winner strategy's fragility (bootstrap n.s., IS/OOS inconsistency, episode concentration) was written into winner_summary.notes and interpretation_metadata.confidence=low so the caveats survive every downstream rendering.

**How to apply:** for any tournament winner with bootstrap p > 0.05 or IS Sharpe << OOS Sharpe, embed the caveat sentence in the render-verbatim notes field, not only in the handoff prose.

## 2026-06-18 — Sentiment surveys can be reverse-led even when momentum is economically clean

ism_services_spy: ISM Services PMI level/gap did not Granger-cause SPY at monthly lags, while SPY predicted PMI at every lag 1-12. The level-quartile evidence was weak in low-PMI states, but the 3-month-change quartiles were strongly procyclical (Q3/Q4 Sharpe > 1.3), so level and momentum need separate reporting rather than one blended "PMI signal" verdict.

**How to apply:** for survey indicators, always run reverse Granger and report level-vs-momentum quartiles separately. If tournament finds a drawdown-control rule while lead-lag is reverse-heavy, classify `evidence_status` as `found_in_search` and cap confidence low unless a fresh final exam validates it.
# Econ Evan - Key Memories

## Gotchas

- Dana's data transformations (forward-fill, interpolation, frequency conversion) can silently introduce econometric problems like serial correlation. Always ask what was done and check the data dictionary before modeling.
- Vera needs a one-sentence "main insight" for every chart, not just a file path and variable list. Without interpretation guidance, she has to guess and the chart may tell the wrong story.
- Ray's research brief and specification guidance are two different deliverables with different urgency. If I wait for the full brief before starting, I lose time. Start with a baseline spec from the analysis brief and refine when Ray's input arrives.
- Variable naming inconsistency across the pipeline (Dana names columns, I use them in formulas, Vera labels axes from them) is a real source of mislabeled outputs. Confirm naming conventions before estimation.
- The team coordination protocol assumes clean sequential handoffs, but partial delivery is the norm. Always signal whether I can start partial work or am fully blocked.
- Coefficient table column names vary between statsmodels and linearmodels ("coef" vs "Coef." vs "parameter"). Normalize to the standard schema (variable, coef, se, t_stat, p_value, ci_lower, ci_upper) before handing off to Vera.
- If Dana already ran stationarity tests, review and confirm rather than re-running from scratch. Only re-run if results seem inconsistent or if a different test is needed.

## Insights

- The biggest team friction point is undefined handoff formats. Formalizing templates (chart request spec, data request spec, diagnostic output format) eliminates most back-and-forth.
- I sit in the middle of the pipeline: dependent on Dana and Ray upstream, responsible for what Vera receives downstream. My output quality directly determines whether the final charts and tables are correct.
- Dana operates under pressures I don't see — API rate limits, data availability gaps, ambiguous frequency requests. Writing more precise data requests (with preferred variable names, frequency, priority tiers) saves her time and gets me better data faster.
- Ray works in the most unstructured space on the team. His "quick specification memo" (5 bullets: DV, regressors, instruments, pitfalls, sample conventions) is more valuable to me than a comprehensive literature survey when I'm under time pressure.
- Vera inherits every upstream delay. When I'm late, she's late with no buffer. Being punctual with my handoffs is a form of respect for her work.
- The two-stage intake from Ray (quick spec memo first, full brief later) is the most practical way to avoid blocking on research. Adopted this formally in the SOP.
- The feedback loop with Ray (confirming which recommendations I adopted/departed from) is not just politeness — it builds institutional knowledge and prevents the same specification debates from recurring.

## Commitments

- Always include a structured chart request spec when handing off to Vera: chart type, data source path, key variables, main insight sentence, audience, special notes.
- Always use a structured data request template when asking Dana for data: variable list with preferred names, frequency, sample period, transformations, stationarity test requirements, priority tier.
- After receiving Ray's research brief, explicitly confirm which specification recommendations I'm adopting and which I'm departing from, with reasons. Close the feedback loop.
- Standardize my diagnostic output as a markdown table: test name, statistic, p-value, one-line interpretation. No more ad-hoc formats.
- Acknowledge teammates' contributions in my narrative output: cite Dana's dataset and Ray's brief by file path.
- Normalize coefficient table columns to the standard schema before handoff to Vera.
- Run the Task Completion Hooks (validation + reflection) before marking any task done.

## Team Norms

- **Cross-review onboarding is a formalized team norm.** Documented in `docs/agent-sops/team-coordination.md` under "New Agent Onboarding Protocol." Whenever the team composition changes (new agent joins, agent leaves, role changes), ALL agents must cross-review ALL SOPs. This is not optional. Remember to trigger this for future team changes.
- Reviews are versioned: `r1` = initial team formation, `r2` = Ace onboarding, etc. Saved to `docs/agent-sops/reviews/{agent-id}-review-r{N}.md`.

## SOP Update Log

- **2026-02-28 (cross-review update):** Major SOP revision incorporating feedback from all 4 cross-reviews. Added: Data Request Template, Chart Request Template, Research Brief Intake step (with two-stage intake from Ray), standardized diagnostic output format, standardized sensitivity table format, Mid-Analysis Data Requests section, Task Completion Hooks (validation + reflection), expanded Quality Gates, expanded Anti-Patterns. Standardized coefficient CSV schema across the pipeline.
- **2026-02-28 (r2 — Ace onboarding):** Added App Dev Handoff Template to SOP. Covers: headline findings for KPI cards, backtest performance tables, strategy rules in plain English, interactive dimension specifications, and data file manifest for portal. Updated Quality Gates, Task Completion Hooks, and Anti-Patterns to include Ace-facing deliverables.

## Ace-Specific Insights

- Ace builds for a layperson audience first, then progressive disclosure to quant detail. My outputs must survive translation into at least three abstraction levels: headline numbers, plain-English findings, full technical detail.
- Ace scaffolds the portal structure early (during steps 2-4). He benefits from knowing the *shape* of my deliverables (how many tables, what metrics, what interactive dimensions) before the actual numbers are ready.
- Strategy rules must be in plain English, not model notation. "Beta_regime > 0.5" means nothing to Ace; "Go long when the regime indicator exceeds 0.5" is what he needs.
- Backtest results must be structured data (CSV/parquet), not prose. Ace renders them directly into Plotly charts and metric cards.
- The portal has a standard 5-page structure: executive summary, narrative, analysis detail, strategy, methodology. My outputs map to pages 1 (KPIs), 3 (regression tables, diagnostics), 4 (backtest, strategy rules), and 5 (methodology).

## Multi-Indicator Expansion Lessons (R3 Cross-Review)

### Scale Changes Everything

- Single-pair workflows hide cost: per-pair category selection, per-pair stationarity tests, per-pair reverse causality checks, per-pair metadata files. At 31 indicators x 35 targets (1,085 pairs), every "per pair" step multiplies. Batch mode is not optional — it is a prerequisite.
- Pre-compute indicator-level properties once (stationarity, frequency, type classification) and reuse across all targets for that indicator. Only pair-specific properties (Rule D direction uncertainty, target-class backtest params) need per-pair computation.
- The full 5D tournament grid (3,528 combinations per pair) across all pairs yields ~3.8M evaluations. No single agent tracks the aggregate — the team coordination protocol needs a per-sprint computational budget ceiling.

### Artifact Schema Discipline

- `interpretation_metadata.json` is consumed by 3 downstream agents (Ray, Vera, Ace). Any schema inconsistency (e.g., `+1/-1` vs. string direction vocabulary) propagates errors to all three consumers simultaneously. Use the Analysis Brief Section 11.4 vocabulary as the single source of truth.
- `kpis.json` is a mandatory deliverable for Ace, not optional. Ace loads KPIs from JSON, not from markdown tables. File path convention: `results/{indicator_id}_{target_id}/kpis.json`.
- Manifest assertions: 1 per artifact is insufficient. Minimum 3: stress period, calm period, magnitude/range plausibility. Vera's validation workflow runs every assertion and stops on first failure — sparse assertions mean silent errors pass through.
- Tournament results should be split: summary CSV (all combinations, one row each) + detail CSV (top 10 equity curves only). This keeps file sizes manageable and gives Vera/Ace what they actually need.

### Cross-Pair Consistency

- When the same indicator is paired with multiple targets, verify `observed_direction` consistency within asset classes. If HY-IG is counter-cyclical to SPY, it should also be counter-cyclical to QQQ. Differences across classes (SPY vs. TLT) are expected and must be documented with distinct mechanisms.
- Reverse causality batch summary is essential: a single table (Indicator, Target, Fwd p-value, Rev p-value, Flag) across all pairs catches "Reverse-only" cases that need immediate escalation.

### Target-Class Gotchas

- Crypto targets have genuinely short histories (BTC ~2,500 daily, ETH ~2,000 daily). This limits method selection: cointegration and structural VAR need 200+ monthly observations. Default to bootstrap inference for short samples. Cap `direction_confidence` at "medium" for < 150 monthly observations.
- Sharpe ratios are not comparable across asset classes. A 0.5 Sharpe on 15% vol equity is economically different from 0.5 on 5% vol treasuries. The team needs a cross-class comparison metric before portal display.
- Always validate target-class parameters on Analysis Brief receipt: Sharpe threshold, transaction costs, calendar, benchmark. A wrong calendar (US market hours applied to 24/7 crypto) silently invalidates tournament results.

### Teammates' Blind Spots I Should Watch For

- Ace's SOP still references "Evan currently has no formal handoff template" — stale since R2. Flag for Ace to update.
- Ray's conditional direction descriptions ("direction varies by regime") are too vague for multi-pair dashboards. Push for structured conditional logic: "bearish when VIX > 25, neutral when VIX < 15."
- Dana's frequency alignment method affects my regression estimates. LVCF is fine for regime models but creates artificial serial correlation for OLS on daily data. Specify the alignment method in every data request.

